\subsection{Graphical Evaluation and Analysis}
To visualize the performance dynamics, the experimental benchmarks are plotted across two complementary dimensions in Fig. \ref{fig:exec_time} and Fig. \ref{fig:density_effect}.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centerline{\includegraphics[width=0.48\textwidth]{exec_time.png}}
\caption{Execution time comparison between Repeated Dijkstra and Floyd-Warshall across all evaluated graph types.}
\label{fig:exec_time}
\end{figure}

Fig. \ref{fig:exec_time} illustrates the overall execution time progression across all test benchmarks. The plot highlights the massive performance divergence in sparse environments: for the Large Sparse benchmark ($V=400$, 10\% edges), Repeated Dijkstra completes in $\approx 0.61$ seconds, whereas Floyd-Warshall requires $\approx 3.99$ seconds. Conversely, as graph density increases to 90\%, Dijkstra's heap operations increase significantly, narrowing the gap. In the Small Dense benchmark ($V=50$, 90\% edges), Floyd-Warshall outperforms Dijkstra ($0.00792$s vs $0.00871$s) due to the absence of priority queue overhead.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centerline{\includegraphics[width=0.48\textwidth]{density_effect.png}}
\caption{Effect of scaling vertex count ($V$) on execution time under sparse (10\%) and dense (90\%) edge conditions.}
\label{fig:density_effect}
\end{figure}

Fig. \ref{fig:density_effect} isolates the direct impact of vertex scaling ($V = 50, 200, 400$) under separate sparse and dense curves. As demonstrated, Floyd-Warshall's curves for sparse and dense graphs remain nearly identical across all scales, confirming its pure $O(V^3)$ density-independent nature. On the other hand, Dijkstra's performance is strictly split: Dijkstra Sparse remains near the bottom axis even at $V=400$, while Dijkstra Dense climbs sharply alongside Floyd-Warshall due to $E \approx V^2$ edge relaxations.

\section{Discussion and Limitations}
\label{sec:discussion}
While our results align closely with theoretical expectations, several limitations bound their generality:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Random Graph Distribution:} All test graphs used uniformly random edge weights and densities. Real-world networks (road maps, social networks) typically exhibit power-law-distributed connectivity, which could shift the observed crossover point.
    \item \textbf{Data Structure Selection:} Our implementation uses a lazy binary-heap Dijkstra without decrease-key support. A Fibonacci-heap variant \cite{fredman1987fibonacci} could narrow or eliminate Dijkstra's disadvantage at high density by reducing the $\log V$ factor, though prior empirical work notes Fibonacci heaps carry high constant-factor overhead in practice \cite{fredman1987fibonacci}.
    \item \textbf{Edge Weight Restrictions:} This study did not test negative edge weights, which Floyd-Warshall handles natively but which would require Johnson's re-weighting technique \cite{johnson1977efficient} for Dijkstra-based approaches.
    \item \textbf{Language Overhead:} All measurements reflect a pure-Python implementation. The relative gap between the two algorithms would likely narrow under a vectorized (NumPy) or compiled implementation, since Floyd-Warshall's simple inner loop benefits disproportionately from low-level optimization compared to Dijkstra's heap control flow.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}
This project successfully implemented and evaluated two solutions for the APSP problem. The empirical results perfectly mirror theoretical time complexities. Dijkstra's algorithm should be the default choice for large, real-world networks (which are typically sparse). However, for small, highly dense graphs, or environments requiring negative weight handling, Floyd-Warshall remains a powerful and simple-to-implement alternative.

